                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Keyboard Killers is a very simple reflex game built around typing fast enough to destroy incoming meteors. I am basically sitting there watching letters appear on the screen and trying to hit the right key before the meteor reaches my ship. The idea is almost ridiculously straightforward, but I actually like how quickly it gets going. There is no story to learn or complicated system to understand. I just start typing and try to get faster.\n\nThere are 28 levels split across four difficulty settings, and each level has its own rock song playing in the background. The later levels become much more demanding, with the amount of typing I need to do increasing to the point where I have to react almost instantly. I like that the difficulty comes from speed rather than from adding loads of complicated mechanics. Once I get into the rhythm, I can find myself concentrating completely on the next letter while trying not to make a mistake.\n\nThe music is probably the part that gives the game more personality than it really needs. Having a different rock track for each level makes the whole thing feel more energetic, especially when the later stages become hectic. There is also a special level where I can create my own music using 22 different sounds. It does not turn the game into a full music creator, but I like having something a little different to mess around with after spending time on the main typing levels.\n\nThe biggest problem for me is that there is not much depth beyond the typing. Once I understand the basic system, I am still doing the same thing for every level. The four difficulty settings make the game harder, but they do not really change how I play. There are 15 achievements as well, including some that require a lot of repeated typing, but they are mostly extra goals rather than meaningful progression. I can see myself enjoying the challenge for a while and then running out of reasons to keep playing once I have had enough of the same basic test.\n\nI also think the game can be a little harsh with the whole keyboard idea. The later stages are intentionally very fast, and I have to type at a really high rate to keep up. That can be satisfying when I am improving, but it can also become tiring on my hands after playing for too long. I would much rather play it in short sessions than sit there trying to grind through every level in one go. The simple presentation also means there is not much else to look at when I am struggling with a difficult section.\n\nWhat I do appreciate is that it is completely free and very easy to understand. I can load it up, play a few levels and close it without having to remember anything for next time. It is also one of those games where I can genuinely feel myself getting better because my typing speed and ability to react quickly matter more than complicated game knowledge. I just wish there was a little more variety around the core idea, because there is a good foundation here that could have supported more modes or challenges.\n\nOverall, Keyboard Killers is a fun little reflex test that works best when I treat it as a quick challenge rather than a full game. I like the fast typing, rock soundtrack and increasing difficulty, and the free price makes the limited content easier for me to accept. The lack of meaningful variety and progression keeps it well away from my higher ratings, but the core idea does work and the later levels can be genuinely challenging. It is simple, direct and surprisingly useful if I want to test how quickly I can react on a keyboard.\n\nRating: 6.7/10\n",
